        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And Canaan saw the nakedness of his father Ham, and he went out and told his two brethren who were without.&#x27; Canaan, not Ham himself, is the verse&#x27;s actor — Jubilees clarifies the narrative ambiguity of Gen 9:22. **Tewahedo Hamitic-identity anchor (paired with Jub 9:13)**: the curse falls on Canaan (a specific bloodline) not on Ham (the broader descent). The Tewahedo reading is explicit: there is NO racial curse on the Hamitic descendants generally; the curse is bounded, juridical, and limited to Canaan&#x27;s line. This is a sharp Tewahedo distinctive against the Western &#x27;curse of Ham&#x27; racialisation.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 7:13 (Noah's commandments), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
