        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And in the seventh year of this jubilee Noah planted vines on the mountain on which the ark had rested, named Lubar, one of the Ararat Mountains, and they produced fruit in the fourth year, and he guarded their fruit, and gathered it in this year in the seventh month&#x27; — Noah&#x27;s vineyard on Mount Lubar. Tewahedo monastic-agriculture tradition (the vine-cultivation at monastic settlements in the Ethiopian highlands) draws on the Noahic vineyard-as-righteous-agriculture motif, though Tewahedo monasticism guards strictly against wine-intemperance (the prohibition Jubilees develops further).</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 7:36 (Noah's life (7:36)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
