        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;In the twenty-ninth jubilee, in the first week, in the beginning thereof, Arpachshad took to himself a wife and her name was Rasu&#x27;eja, the daughter of Susan, the daughter of Elam, and she bare him a son in the third year in this week, and he called his name Kainam.&#x27; Post-flood genealogical reconstruction — Arpachshad&#x27;s son Kainam (Cainan) is included, matching the Septuagint and Tewahedo Bible against the Masoretic. **Tewahedo textual anchor**: the inclusion of Cainan between Arpachshad and Shelah (also Lk 3:36) is preserved in Jubilees, the LXX, and the Tewahedo Bible — a triple canonical witness against the Masoretic abridgement.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 8:1 (Geographic division), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
