        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And he took to himself a wife and her name was Melka, the daughter of Madai, the son of Japheth, and in the fourth year he begat a son, and called his name Shelah, for, he said: Truly I have been sent.&#x27; Shelah&#x27;s name etymologised as &#x27;sent&#x27; — naming is theologically charged. **Tewahedo onomastic anchor**: Tewahedo baptismal-naming tradition draws on the Jubilees pattern of theologically-significant names assigned at birth; the practice of giving a Christian (often patristic or Marian) name at baptism participates in this Jubilees onomastic register.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 8:12 (Geographic division), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
