        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And the chief of the spirits, Mastema, came and said: Lord, Creator, let some of them remain before me, and let them hearken to my voice, and do all that I shall say unto them; for if some of them are not left to me, I shall not be able to execute the power of my will on the sons of men&#x27; — Mastema&#x27;s petition for demonic remnant. Tewahedo demonology preserves Mastema as canonical: the post-Flood demonic presence is divinely permitted, bounded, and serves a probative function. This makes Tewahedo theodicy distinctively non-dualistic: evil is BOUNDED, not autonomous.</p></aside>',
