        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And in the thirty-third jubilee, in the first year in the second week, Peleg took to himself a wife, whose name was Lōmnā the daughter of Sina&#x27;ar, and she bare him a son in the fourth year of this week, and he called his name Reu; for he said: Behold the children of men have become evil through the wicked purpose of building for themselves a city and a tower in the land of Shinar.&#x27; The Tower-of-Babel narrative is introduced as a CORPORATE evil — the building of a CITY (not merely a tower) signals organised human autonomy against divine ordinance. **Tewahedo political-theology anchor**: the Tewahedo reading of the Tower as a city-state-building project grounds the canonical critique of imperial autonomy — the legitimate political form is the Noahide-allotted nation, not the post-Babel hegemonic city.</p></aside>',
