        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And they made for themselves molten images, and they worshipped each the idol the molten image which they had made for themselves, and they began to make graven images and unclean simulacra&#x27; — Jubilees traces the cascade of post-Babel apostasy through Serug&#x27;s generation, presenting idolatry not as a single fall but as a slow societal drift. Tewahedo catechesis (Andəmta on Genesis 11) inherits this gradualist diagnosis: sin is taught, learned, and inherited within communities, so the antidote — Abram&#x27;s break, then Sinai, then Christ&#x27;s gathered ecclesia — likewise advances by community-formation, not isolated individual conversion.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 11:4 (Abraham cycle (11:4)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
