        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And Serug grew up and dwelt in Ur of the Chaldees, near to the father of his wife&#x27;s mother, and he worshipped idols&#x27; — Jubilees marks the precise generation in which the rot becomes nominal: Abram&#x27;s great-grandfather Serug actively serves the idols of his in-laws. The Tewahedo Andəmta exegesis on Abram&#x27;s election reads this verse as proof that elective grace stands against inherited line: God draws Abram from a pedigree of idolators, not from an unbroken righteous succession — a key anti-Pelagian and anti-genealogical-merit anchor in Tewahedo soteriology.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 11:8 (Abraham cycle (11:8)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
