        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And the child began to understand the errors of the earth that all went astray after graven images and after uncleanness, and his father taught him writing, and he was two weeks of years old, and he separated himself from his father, that he might not worship idols with him&#x27; — young Abram rejects his father&#x27;s idolatry. Tewahedo missiological tradition cites this verse as the canonical witness that conversion-from-idolatry CAN happen even within a thoroughly pagan household — a foundational warrant for evangelism into traditional-religious contexts.</p></aside>',
