        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And in the third week of that jubilee the unclean ravens came and devoured the seed which had been sown in the earth in order to destroy the earth&#x27; — Jubilees portrays the ravens as Mastema&#x27;s instruments sent to ruin the agricultural blessing. Tewahedo readers see in the boy-Abram who drives away the ravens (11:19-21) the prototype of the priest who guards the offered seed; the Tewahedo blessing of the fields and the kahn (priestly) intercession against locust and crop-loss draw their canonical warrant from this passage. Abram is presented as priest-king before Mosaic ordination — anchoring the Tewahedo doctrine of universal pre-Mosaic priesthood from which the kǝhnät descends.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 11:18 (Abraham cycle (11:18)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
