        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;My father, what help or profit have we from those idols which thou dost worship and before which thou dost bow thyself? For there is no spirit in them, for they are dumb forms, and a misleading of the heart&#x27; — Abram&#x27;s argument to Terah is preserved in Jubilees as a model of filial-yet-uncompromising witness. The Tewahedo monastic instruction tradition (Mäwasǝʾǝt) cites this verse as the pattern for confronting elders in error: respectful address (&#x27;my father&#x27;) joined to plain doctrinal refutation. The Andəmta commentary frames it as the first canonical instance of a son catechizing his father — a permitted reversal where truth requires.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 12:4 (Abraham cycle (12:4)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
