        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;My son, hold thy peace, lest they slay thee&#x27; — Terah confesses privately to Abram that he too knows the gods are nothing but fears the community will kill them for saying so. Jubilees here exposes the social mechanism by which apostasy is enforced: not conviction but terror. Tewahedo persecution-memory (the Gragn Ahmed wars of the 1530s; martyrdoms under Italian fascism in 1936-37) reads this verse as the canonical warrant for distinguishing private faith from public witness, while still upholding the latter as the higher form — Abram refuses to be silenced (12:8-14).</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 12:7 (Abraham cycle (12:7)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
