        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And in the night Abram arose and burned the house of the idols, and he burned all that was in the house, and no man knew it. And they arose in the night and sought to save their gods from the midst of the fire. And Haran hasted to save them, but the fire flamed over him, and he was burned in the fire, and he died in Ur of the Chaldees before Terah his father&#x27; — Jubilees attributes Haran&#x27;s death (Gen 11:28) to a failed rescue of idols from Abram&#x27;s iconoclasm. Tewahedo readings of the Gragn destruction of churches (1530s) and of contemporary church-burning in northern Ethiopia find canonical anchor here: those who serve images perish with them; the iconographic-but-not-idolatrous Tewahedo veneration is sharply distinguished from the literal-idol category Abram destroyed.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 12:12 (Abraham cycle (12:12)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
