        "Tewahedo — Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)",
        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And he built there an altar, and he offered thereon a burnt-offering to the Lord who had appeared unto him, and he called upon the name of the Lord&#x27; — at Bethel, prior to all Mosaic legislation, Abram constitutes the first patriarchal altar. The Tewahedo Liturgy of Abraham (Anaphora of the Patriarchs, used in periodic monastic-festal commemoration) draws its canonical warrant from this verse: legitimate altar precedes Mosaic priestly ordination by centuries, anchoring the Tewahedo conviction that the Christian altar is patriarchal-prototypical, not merely a Mosaic-replacement.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 13:8 (Abraham cycle (13:8)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
