        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)",
        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And Abram returned to the place where he had at the first made the altar, and he called there on the name of the Lord, and said: Thou art my God, the Eternal God&#x27; — after the Egypt-sojourn (13:11-15), Abram returns to the Bethel altar. Jubilees inscribes return-to-altar as the canonical post-failure rhythm. Tewahedo confession-practice (näsḫa) draws on this pattern: the penitent returns precisely to the same altar previously profaned, not to a substitute. The Andǝmta names this &#x27;Abram&#x27;s return&#x27; (mätsä Abram) as the type of restoration after Diaspora exile.</p></aside>',
