        "Tewahedo — Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)",
        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And in the fourth year thereof Isaac took to himself a wife, and her name was Rebecca the daughter of Bethuel, the son of Milcah, the sister of Laban and father of Rebecca, the son of Nahor, the brother of Abraham, the sister of Isaac his wife&#x27; — Jubilees follows the elaborate kinship geometry of Gen 24 to emphasize that Rebekah is a covenant-line spouse, not a Canaanite. Tewahedo marriage canon (zǝmd zǝr) draws on this verse for the kinship-careful pattern of Christian marriage: blood-kinship within bounds is positively desired (against random outside marriage that risks doctrinal-and-cultural drift), while incest is forbidden. The Tewahedo practical pastoral norm — endogamy preferred within Orthodox community boundaries — has its canonical warrant in the patriarchal practice preserved by Jubilees.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 19:13 (Sarah's death + Rebekah (19:13)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
