        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And, my children, observe the commandment of Abraham your father, and be not separated from the worship of the Lord. And, my children, beware of all whoredom and uncleanness, and of all pollution of sin&#x27; — Abraham&#x27;s testament forbids whoredom, idolatrous association, and ritual impurity as a triad. The Tewahedo monastic-and-lay catechesis preserves this triad as the negative-ethical core of inheritance: each child of the covenant must avoid sexual impurity, idolatrous syncretism, and ritual pollution. The Tewahedo täzkär (memorial) for the departed includes a renewal of this Abrahamic charge — the deceased is commended to the next generation as a witness to the three negative bounds.</p></aside>',
