        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And as regards the blood of all flesh that ascendeth on the altar, place ye salt thereon; and as for the blood, eat it not, for it is the soul; and let no blood be left in the flesh which thou eatest&#x27; — Abraham&#x27;s priestly instructions to Isaac repeat the no-blood-consumption command (Jub 6:7, 7:34 antecedents). Tewahedo dietary law preserves the no-blood rule with priestly emphasis: the canonical-Tewahedo prohibition against eating blood (drawn from Gen 9:4 + Lev 17 + Acts 15 + this Jubilees triple-witness) marks the Tewahedo apart from most Western Christian practice. The salt-on-blood prescription is preserved in Tewahedo altar practice.</p></aside>',
