        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And there is no greater sin in His sight than this — to eat the blood of any flesh; and they that eat shall be rooted out of the land&#x27; — Abraham frames blood-consumption as a sin of uniquely-rooting-out severity. Tewahedo penitential canon (mäṣḥafä qəddase confession-list) catalogues unintentional blood-consumption (improperly bled meat) as a sin requiring specific priestly absolution. The Tewahedo&#x27;s distinctive abstention from non-kosher-style butchery without specific priestly blessing of the slaughter draws canonical warrant from Jub 21:18 — the prohibition is presented as a category-of-its-own, separate from the general dietary code.</p></aside>',
