        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And do thou, my son, observe His commandments and His ordinances and His judgments, and walk not after the abominations, and after the graven images, and after the molten images&#x27; — Abraham&#x27;s closing charge to Isaac: covenant-faithfulness expressed positively (keep the commandments) and negatively (no idolatry in any form). The Tewahedo iconographic tradition has been careful to distinguish itself from this prohibition: Tewahedo ikons are NOT graven images (they are painted boards, not sculpted statues; venerated as windows-to-the-real, not worshipped as substitutes for it). The patriarchal warrant for the Tewahedo iconography-yes-statues-no boundary is implicit in this verse&#x27;s careful threefold listing of forbidden artifact types: abominations (idolatrous practices), graven images (sculpture), molten images (cast metal) — none of which describe the Tewahedo painted ikon.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 21:22 (Abraham's priestly instructions (21:22)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
