        "Tewahedo — Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)",
        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And the words of Esau, her elder son, were told to Rebecca in a dream, and Rebecca sent and called Jacob her younger son, and said unto him: behold, Esau thy brother will take vengeance on thee so as to kill thee&#x27; — Jubilees uniquely makes Rebekah&#x27;s foreknowledge of Esau&#x27;s murderous plot a divinely-given dream, not mere intuition. The Tewahedo dream-discernment tradition (the matriarchal-prophetic dream that saves the patriarch is a recognized canonical category) draws warrant: legitimate dreams from God can be relied on for major decisions when corroborated by the dreamer&#x27;s righteous standing and the moral content of the dream. Rebekah&#x27;s dream meets both tests.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 27:1 (Jacob's flight and Bethel vision (27:1)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
