        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And he set his face toward Haran, and he was alone in the way, and he travelled three days, and went to Lūz... And he tarried there one night, and he took a stone of the place and set it under his head, and he was alone in the place, and there was none with him&#x27; — Jacob&#x27;s departure to Haran. Tewahedo monastic-pilgrimage theology preserves the alone-with-stone-pillow image as the canonical pattern of the ascetic&#x27;s solitary travel: pillow of stone, hearing of theophany.</p></aside>',
