        "Tewahedo — Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)",
        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And on the new moon of the first month, the Lord said unto Jacob: arise, and go up to Bethel, and dwell there, and make there an altar to the God who appeared to thee, who fled from the face of Esau thy brother&#x27; — Jubilees dates Jacob&#x27;s return to Bethel to the new moon of the first month (eve of Passover). The Tewahedo theme of return-to-the-place-of-vision (the disciple returns to the locus of initial calling for re-consecration) finds canonical-temporal anchor: the new-moon-of-the-first-month date links Jacob&#x27;s Bethel re-consecration to the Passover-Pascha cluster of dates — return is liturgically tied to Christ&#x27;s Paschal mystery for the Tewahedo reader.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 31:1 (Isaac's visit to Bethel (31:1)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
