        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)",
        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And Jacob said unto Esau: hast thou not made the oath with me, even my father, and these our nine fathers? Why didst thou come now to dishonour us by reason of thy sons coming up against us with a multitude?&#x27; — Esau is confronted with his violation of the Jub 35:9 oath (which he swore to Rebekah on her deathbed and to Isaac at Jub 36:18). The Tewahedo doctrine of oath-binding (the qǝddase oath has eternal-vertical weight) finds canonical anchor here: oaths sworn to a dying parent are inviolable; the treacherous-relative is rendered legitimately-engageable by his own oath-breaking, not by Jacob&#x27;s choice. Tewahedo monastic vow-tradition treats vow-breaking as the canonical-equivalent of Esau&#x27;s treachery.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 37:25 (Esau-Jacob war (37:25)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
