        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And Judah&#x27;s sons drew their bows, and shot at Adoram and slew him; and Levi took Reuben aside, and they sent against the men who were on the right and the left, and they slew them&#x27; — Jubilees credits Judah and Levi specifically with the battlefield victory over Esau&#x27;s forces. The Tewahedo Solomonic-dynasty (descending from Judah through David) preserves a memory of the Judah-as-battlefield-leader patriarchal-canonical pattern: the Tewahedo king (nəguś) takes the field with the priest (kǝhnät) accompanying — the Judah-Levi battlefield partnership is the canonical-patriarchal antecedent of the Tewahedo king-and-priest joint-leadership pattern at moments of national crisis.</p></aside>',
