        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And after these things, in the third year of this week, the wife of his master lifted up her eyes and looked at Joseph, and she loved him&#x27; — Jubilees follows the Gen 39 sequence with the Potiphar&#x27;s-wife temptation. Tewahedo monastic-chastity formation reads this verse as the canonical pattern: external temptation comes first as the look-of-the-eyes (the visual seizure precedes the verbal proposition). The Tewahedo desert-fathers&#x27; discipline of custody-of-the-eyes (mäḫläla aʿyəntä) finds canonical anchor in the Joseph-narrative&#x27;s careful presentation of the temptation sequence: eyes → words → propositions → physical confrontation, each stage offering a distinct opportunity for refusal.</p></aside>',
