        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And Joseph remembered the Lord and the words which Jacob, his father, used to read from amongst the words of Abraham, that no man should commit fornication with a woman who hath a husband, that for him the punishment of death hath been ordained in the heavens before the Most High Lord&#x27; — Joseph&#x27;s refusal is grounded in the patriarchal-canonical pre-Mosaic adultery law from Abraham (cited as the words &#x27;Jacob used to read&#x27;). Tewahedo doctrine of cumulative-canonical formation: Joseph the young man can refuse adultery because he has been catechized by his father from his grandfather&#x27;s words — the canonical-pedagogical chain Abraham → Jacob → Joseph is the canonical anchor for the Tewahedo family-catechism tradition. The Tewahedo father&#x27;s responsibility to read patriarchal-canonical books to his children before puberty is canonically rooted here.</p></aside>',
