        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And the king commanded that they should arouse Joseph out of the prison: and they arose him out of the prison, and shaved him, and clothed him with new raiment, and presented him before the king&#x27; — Jubilees preserves the Gen 41:14 raising-from-prison narrative. Tewahedo Holy-Week Friday-into-Saturday-into-Sunday liturgical pattern reads this as the canonical sequence: Joseph is raised from the pit-prison (cf. tomb), shaved (cf. body-preparation), clothed with new raiment (cf. resurrection-glorification), presented before the king (cf. ascension). The four-fold raised-shaved-clothed-presented sequence is preserved typologically in Tewahedo Easter-day liturgy: the priest enacts each stage in the Resurrection commemoration.</p></aside>',
