        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And the king said unto Joseph: I have dreamed two dreams, and there is no man who can interpret them. And now I have heard concerning thee, that when thou hearest dreams thou dost interpret them&#x27; — Pharaoh acknowledges Joseph&#x27;s wisdom-from-the-Lord. Jubilees presents Joseph as the canonical-OT example of Spirit-wisdom-given-to-the-rejected-righteous: the prison-vindicated dreamer becomes the interpreter for the king who cannot understand his own dreams. The Tewahedo monastic discernment-tradition (märǝḥǝbǝr — &#x27;spiritual fatherhood&#x27; charism) draws on this canonical OT pattern: the genuinely-Spirit-led-interpreter sees what the worldly-king cannot, and the worldly-king must acknowledge his dependence on Spirit-given counsel.</p></aside>',
