        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And he gave him to wife Asenath, the daughter of Potiphar, the priest of Heliopolis, the chief cook&#x27; — Jubilees preserves Joseph&#x27;s marriage to Asenath, the daughter of Potiphar the priest of Heliopolis (later Egyptian apocryphal-Jewish literature substantially develops Asenath&#x27;s conversion). The Tewahedo tradition of canonical-conversion of Gentile-spouse: Joseph&#x27;s wife is a Gentile-pagan-priest&#x27;s daughter, yet she becomes the mother of Ephraim and Manasseh (Israel&#x27;s continuation). The Tewahedo&#x27;s distinctive pastoral practice of welcoming and catechizing the Gentile-or-non-Orthodox spouse of an Orthodox member into full sacramental fellowship has its OT canonical anchor in Asenath&#x27;s reception.</p></aside>',
