        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And in the forty-fifth jubilee in the second week and in the second year, Judah took for his first-born Er, a wife from the daughters of Aram, named Tamar&#x27; — Jubilees&#x27; framing of the Judah-Tamar narrative with the canon&#x27;s typical chronological-jubilee structure. The verse demonstrates Jubilees&#x27; distinctive editorial method: re-narrating Genesis with jubilee-week timestamps. Tewahedo chronological tradition preserves this jubilee-counting as a way of dating biblical events.</p></aside>',
