        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And in the fifth year Tamar was reckoned unto Onan, his second son, that he should take her, and Onan said: I will not give my seed to my brother, the seed of Tamar — and the Lord slew him before my face for the wickedness which he did, and slew Onan also for the same&#x27; — Jubilees preserves Onan&#x27;s sin of refusing to raise up seed for Tamar (Gen 38:9). Tewahedo doctrine on the obligation of the brother-in-law to raise up seed for the dead brother (the yǝbäm or levirate-relative obligation) finds canonical anchor here: the obligation is so weighty that its refusal is named alongside the worst-category covenant violations. The Tewahedo modern canonical-pastoral practice of supporting widows from within the extended family draws warrant from this passage.</p></aside>',
