        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And we showed to Judah that he should not seek her any more, because of the judgment of the Holy of holies; and Tamar was acquitted, and she became more righteous than he. And he had relations with her no more&#x27; — Judah&#x27;s confession &#x27;she is more righteous than I&#x27; (Gen 38:26) is preserved with explicit divine ratification: Tamar is canonically vindicated, Judah is canonically rebuked. Tewahedo penitential canon reads this as the canonical pattern for the confession-of-the-stronger-sinner-against-the-weaker-victim: Judah&#x27;s straightforward acknowledgment &#x27;she is more righteous than I&#x27; is the model formula for confession when the wronged party turns out to be canonically-just. The Tewahedo confessor&#x27;s näsḫa-of-acknowledgment formula draws verbal anchor here.</p></aside>',
