        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And in the first year of the third week of the forty-fifth jubilee a famine began to come into the land, and the rain refused to be given to the earth, for none whatever fell&#x27; — Jubilees preserves the Gen 42:1 famine context. The Tewahedo agrarian-monastic tradition reads famine-narratives canonically: corporate suffering is the divine instrument for canonical-reunification (Jacob&#x27;s family is reunified through famine-driven travel). The Tewahedo täzkar-ǝgziʾabəḥer (commemoration of God) tradition during years of agricultural distress draws on the Joseph-famine pattern: collective suffering as the matrix of providential mercy preparing reconciliation.</p></aside>',
