        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And he commanded that he should keep one of them in prison, but the others he sent away with corn, and he said: bring Benjamin to me&#x27; — Joseph&#x27;s demand for Benjamin&#x27;s presence as the condition for the brothers&#x27; return preserves the Gen 42 narrative. Tewahedo canonical-reading: the demand-for-Benjamin is functionally the demand-for-Jacob&#x27;s-fully-trusting-the-arrangement, since Benjamin is Jacob&#x27;s last-remaining-child-of-Rachel and is dearer than even Joseph was. The Tewahedo pastoral pattern of asking the penitent to entrust the most precious thing as the canonical-test of repentance draws on this canonical OT pattern.</p></aside>',
