        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And in the third year of that week Jacob sent his sons to Egypt to buy them food, and Benjamin went with them&#x27; — Jacob finally consents to Benjamin&#x27;s going to Egypt. Tewahedo discernment-tradition reads this verse as the canonical pattern of the father&#x27;s permission-after-resistance: the patriarchal-father initially refuses, the canonical circumstances tighten, the refusal yields to entrustment — the entrustment is canonical-discipline-yielding-providential-good. The Tewahedo gədl (saintly-biography) genre traces this pattern in Tewahedo saints&#x27; lives: initial resistance to a divine call yields to consent through canonical-discipline, and the consent then becomes vehicle of providential good.</p></aside>',
