        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And he commanded the steward of his house: hide my silver cup in the sack of my brother Benjamin; and let his money for the corn be also therein&#x27; — Joseph&#x27;s silver-cup ruse-test of his brothers&#x27; loyalty to Benjamin. Tewahedo penitential canon reads this canonically: Joseph&#x27;s ruse is not a sin (deception-with-good-end is permitted to the wronged-righteous-party) but a canonical-permission. The Tewahedo confessor&#x27;s-ruse tradition (the confessor may pose hypothetical scenarios or staged tests to discern the penitent&#x27;s true disposition before granting full absolution) draws canonical warrant from this Joseph-canonical-test pattern.</p></aside>',
