        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And in the first year of the third week in the forty-fifth jubilee, in the second month, Jacob went down to Egypt, his sons with him, and they came to the well of the oath in the seventh of this second month, and he tarried there seven days&#x27; — Jacob deliberately tarries seven days at Beersheba (well of the oath) before proceeding to Egypt. Jubilees uniquely preserves the seven-day delay as a canonical-liturgical pause. Tewahedo monastic-departure tradition: before any major journey or transition, a seven-day prayerful pause at a place of patriarchal-altar memory (Beersheba was Abraham&#x27;s altar of the well of the oath, Gen 21:33; Jub 24:25). The Tewahedo seven-day-week is canonically a unit of canonical-discernment-pause before action — pattern rooted in Jacob&#x27;s pause.</p></aside>',
