        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And the Lord appeared to him in a vision in the night, and said unto him: Jacob, Jacob. And he said: behold, here am I. And He said: I am the God of thy fathers, the God of Abraham, and Isaac; fear not to go down into Egypt, for I will make thee there into a great people. I will go down with thee, and I will bring thee up, and in this land shalt thou be buried, and Joseph shall put his hand upon thy eyes&#x27; — God explicitly promises to accompany Jacob to Egypt and to bring him back. Tewahedo Immanuel-typology reads this as the canonical-OT proof of God&#x27;s accompanying-presence with his people-in-exile: &#x27;I will go down with thee.&#x27; The Tewahedo distinctive ecclesiology of God-with-the-Diaspora — extending to the Tewahedo&#x27;s own historical experience of dispersion under persecution — draws on this verse. The &#x27;Joseph shall put his hand upon thy eyes&#x27; detail anchors the Tewahedo deathbed-ritual of the eldest-son closing the parent&#x27;s eyes at the moment of death.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 44:5 (Jacob to Egypt (44:5)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
