        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And Jacob blessed Pharaoh, and Jacob and his sons dwelt in the land of Goshen, in the best part of the land of Egypt, and Joseph nourished them&#x27; — Jacob blesses Pharaoh (Gen 47:7, 10). The patriarch-blessing-the-Gentile-king pattern is canonical-Tewahedo political-theology: the Orthodox patriarch (in the Tewahedo case, the abun or patriarch of the Ethiopian Church) may legitimately bless the civil-political ruler — even when the ruler is non-Orthodox or non-Christian — because civil authority is divinely ordained (Rom 13:1). The Tewahedo coronation-prayer tradition (the abun&#x27;s blessing of the newly-crowned nəguś) draws canonical-patriarchal warrant from Jacob&#x27;s blessing of Pharaoh.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 45:13 (Jacob settled in Egypt (45:13)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
