        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And Jacob blessed his son Joseph, and the sons of Joseph, and ate the bread of Egypt with him during seventeen years; and he served Joseph as a father in everything according to his desire&#x27; — Jubilees uniquely emphasizes Jacob &#x27;served Joseph as a father in everything&#x27;: the patriarchal-father subordinates himself to his providentially-elevated son for the rest of his life. Tewahedo monastic-humility tradition: the spiritual father is not above receiving spiritual fatherhood from his own child where the child&#x27;s office requires it — Jacob is Joseph&#x27;s biological father but accepts Joseph&#x27;s authority as Egypt&#x27;s governor. The Tewahedo distinctive practice of the elder-bishop bowing to a younger-but-higher-ranking bishop in canonical-liturgical-procession draws warrant from Jacob&#x27;s example.</p></aside>',
