        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And it came to pass that after Jacob died the children of Israel multiplied in the land of Egypt, and they became a great nation, and they were all of one heart, so that brother loved brother and every man helped his brother, and they increased abundantly and multiplied exceedingly, even for ten weeks of years&#x27; — the proto-ecclesial harmony of Israel in early Egypt. Tewahedo ecclesiological theology (the goal of one-heart-and-one-soul, parallel to Acts 4:32) reads this verse as the OT type: harmonious community precedes persecution in the divine pattern, just as church-harmony before persecution.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 46:1 (Egypt sojourn (46:1)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
