        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And he that knew not Joseph reigned in the land of Egypt, and went forth from Memphis, and entered into Goshen, and spoke unto the Israelites in his own language. And he multiplied them and the king persecuted them&#x27; — Jubilees preserves the Ex 1:8 &#x27;new king arose who knew not Joseph&#x27; moment as the canonical onset of Egyptian persecution. Tewahedo persecution-memory: every generation of Tewahedo Christians has known the &#x27;king who knew not Joseph&#x27; figure — the persecutor who has lost the memory of an earlier covenant-of-mutual-benefit. The canonical category &#x27;king who knew not Joseph&#x27; is preserved in Tewahedo theological lexicon as the type of the cyclical-amnesiac-persecutor.</p></aside>',
