        "Tewahedo — Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)",
        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And the king of Egypt went forth against the Canaanites with chariots and a multitude of horsemen and with much people, and he smote them and slew them all, and pursued them as far as the borders of Canaan&#x27; — Jubilees uniquely preserves a battlefield account of the persecuting Pharaoh&#x27;s wider military campaigns. The pattern (the persecutor of the covenant-people is also a wider regional aggressor) is canonically established. Tewahedo political-theology distinguishes legitimate-defensive-war from imperial-aggressive-war: the pharaoh of the persecution is also the pharaoh of regional-imperial-aggression. The two are not coincidentally co-occurring but causally linked: the covenant-amnesiac is also the regional-imperial-aggressor. The Tewahedo&#x27;s resistance to Mussolini&#x27;s 1936 invasion was framed in exactly this canonical category — the imperial-aggressor of 1936 was also the persecutor-of-Tewahedo-Christianity in occupied territory.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 46:14 (Joseph dies; slavery begins (46:14)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
