        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)",
        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And thy mother hid thee three months, and they told regarding her. And she made an ark for thee, and covered it with pitch and asphalt, and placed it in the flags on the bank of the river, and she placed thee in it three days, and thy sister Miriam guarded thee from a distance&#x27; — Jubilees preserves the Ex 2:1-4 Moses-in-the-ark narrative with the explicit three-day-detail. The Tewahedo Moses-as-Christ-type tradition reads the three-day-ark-in-the-water as the canonical OT prefiguration of Christ&#x27;s three-day burial. The &#x27;placed three days&#x27; detail (which is not in Exodus) anchors the typological reading: Moses-in-the-ark for three days = Christ-in-the-tomb for three days. Tewahedo Easter-vigil liturgy preserves the Moses-ark / Christ-tomb correspondence in the readings.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 47:5 (Moses' birth (47:5)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
