        "Tewahedo — Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)",
        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And the man who is clean, and does not come to observe it on the occasion of its day, so as to bring an acceptable offering before the Lord, and to eat and to drink before the Lord on the day of its festival, that man who is clean and close at hand shall be cut off; because he offered not the oblation of the Lord in its appointed season, he shall take the guilt upon himself&#x27; — Passover-attendance is non-negotiable. Tewahedo Fasika practice preserves the obligation-to-attend doctrine: the Easter observance is canonically required participation, not optional devotion.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 49:9 (Passover (49:9)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
