        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And let the children of Israel come and observe the Passover on the day of its fixed time, on the fourteenth day of the first month, between the evenings, from the third part of the day to the third part of the night; for two portions of the day are given to the light, and a third part to the evening&#x27; — Jubilees fixes the precise time of Passover observance (third-of-the-day to third-of-the-night). Tewahedo Fasika preserves the precise canonical-temporal observance with the same threefold-day structuring. The &#x27;two portions of the day given to the light, a third part to the evening&#x27; canonical-cosmology informs the Tewahedo liturgical-day reckoning: light-portions and evening-portion are canonically structured into the Tewahedo daily-office (matins + sext + vespers, three offices precisely matching the canonical-thirds).</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 49:10 (Passover (49:10)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
