        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;And let the children of Israel observe it, and celebrate it from year to year throughout all their generations, once in the year, on the fourteenth day of the first month, between the evenings, in the second week. And no man may eat it in the morning, nor in any time of the day, save in the time of the night until the rising of the sun. And the man that observeth it on the fourteenth day in the evening, he shall acquit himself of his guilt&#x27; — Passover observance acquits-of-guilt: the canonical Passover is canonically-atoning. Tewahedo Fasika theology preserves this: the proper observance of the canonical-Passover IS itself the canonical-atonement-for-the-year&#x27;s-sin. The Tewahedo&#x27;s distinctive emphasis on liturgical-act-AS-atonement (against any &#x27;mere commemoration&#x27; reading of the eucharist) draws warrant from Jub 49:15: the observance itself acquits, not just the recollection-of-the-historical-event.</p></aside>',
        "Jubilees 49:15 (Passover (49:15)), trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Jubilees (Oxford: Clarendon, 1902). PD.",
