        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;But the Sabbath day of the Lord thy God ye shall not do any work thereon, but that which ye have prepared for yourselves on the sixth day, so as to eat, and drink, and rest, and keep Sabbath from all work on that day, and to bless the Lord your God, who has given you a day of festival, and a holy day; and a day of the holy kingdom for all Israel is this day among their days for ever&#x27; — Jubilees inscribes the Sabbath as &#x27;a day of the holy kingdom for all Israel&#x27; — a day liturgically tasting the Kingdom of God in advance. Tewahedo Saturday-Sabbath theology (uniquely among major Christian communions in preserving Saturday-Sabbath alongside Sunday-Lord&#x27;s-Day) reads the Sabbath as canonical foretaste-of-the-Kingdom. The Tewahedo monastic Saturday-vigil tradition (more elaborate than Sunday in some monastic communities) draws warrant from Jub 50:9&#x27;s &#x27;holy kingdom day&#x27; framing.</p></aside>',
