        "Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition) (-150).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Jubilees (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of Jubilees (Mäṣḥafä Kufāle)</em> <small>(c. 150 BC)</small><p>&#x27;Whoever does any work thereon, or goes a journey, or tills (his) farm whether in his house or any other place, and whoever lights a fire, or rides on any beast, or travels by ship on the sea, and whoever strikes or kills anything, or slaughters a beast or a bird, or whoever catches an animal or a bird or a fish, or whoever fasts or makes war on the Sabbaths: the man who does any of these things on the Sabbath shall die&#x27; — Jubilees gives the strictest possible Sabbath-prohibition list, with the canonical &#x27;shall die&#x27; formula. The Tewahedo Saturday-Sabbath observance preserves this strictness in canonical-pastoral form: Saturday is a day of complete rest, abstention from agricultural-and-commercial work, and elevated-spiritual-focus. The Tewahedo&#x27;s distinctive observation of BOTH Saturday (canonical-OT Sabbath) and Sunday (Christ&#x27;s-resurrection-Lord&#x27;s-Day) gives the Tewahedo the canonical-fullness of weekly-time-sanctification, anchored in Jub 50.</p></aside>',
